{"term":"polyamorous","lang":"en","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"identities_orientations","subcategory":"relationship_structure","concept":"polyamory","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"subculture","crude":8,"technical":35,"recognition_pct_current":80,"recognition_pct_peak":80,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Describing a person who practices or is open to consensual romantic or sexual relationships with multiple partners simultaneously.","english_term":"polyamorous","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"Adjectival form of polyamory; both terms surged in mainstream recognition in the 2010s–2020s; distinct from swinging in its emphasis on emotional intimacy alongside or instead of recreational sex."}
{"term":"polyamory","lang":"en","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"identities_orientations","subcategory":"relationship_structure","concept":"polyamory","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"subculture","crude":8,"technical":35,"recognition_pct_current":80,"recognition_pct_peak":80,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"The practice of having consensual romantic or sexual relationships with multiple partners simultaneously.","english_term":"polyamory","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"Coined circa 1990; Greek/Latin hybrid portmanteau (polys + amor); distinct from polygamy (not marriage-based, gender-neutral); distinct from swinging (emphasis on relationships, not just sex)."}
{"term":"monogamous","lang":"en","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"identities_orientations","subcategory":"relationship_structure","concept":"monogamy","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"clinical","crude":0,"technical":60,"recognition_pct_current":93,"recognition_pct_peak":93,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Maintaining a sexual or romantic relationship with only one partner at a time.","english_term":"monogamous","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"The cultural and legal default in most Western societies; used in clinical, legal, and casual registers without distinction; increasing use in contrast with polyamory discourse."}
{"term":"polygamous","lang":"en","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"identities_orientations","subcategory":"relationship_structure","concept":"polygamy","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"clinical","crude":0,"technical":60,"recognition_pct_current":88,"recognition_pct_peak":88,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Having multiple spouses simultaneously; typically illegal in Western jurisdictions but practiced in some religious and cultural communities.","english_term":"polygamous","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"Technically encompasses polygyny (one man, multiple wives) and polyandry (one woman, multiple husbands); in common English usage almost always implies polygyny; carries varying moral weight depending on cultural context."}
{"term":"swinger","lang":"en","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"kink_paraphilia","subcategory":"relationship_structure","concept":"swinging","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_neutral","crude":20,"technical":8,"recognition_pct_current":90,"recognition_pct_peak":90,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"A person who engages in consensual partner-swapping or group sexual activity with other couples.","english_term":"swinger","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"Term became culturally prominent in the 1960s–70s; still active and broadly recognized; distinct from polyamory in its typical emphasis on recreational sex over emotional intimacy; associated with a recognizable subculture with its own venues."}
{"term":"swinging","lang":"en","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"kink_paraphilia","subcategory":"relationship_structure","concept":"swinging","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_neutral","crude":20,"technical":8,"recognition_pct_current":88,"recognition_pct_peak":88,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"The practice of consensual partner-swapping or group sex among couples, typically as a recreational lifestyle.","english_term":"swinging","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"Activity-noun form of swinger; the lifestyle aspect distinguishes it from one-off encounters; still maintains a distinct subculture separate from the newer polyamory community."}
{"term":"open relationship","lang":"en","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"identities_orientations","subcategory":"relationship_structure","concept":"open_relationship","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_neutral","crude":8,"technical":20,"recognition_pct_current":92,"recognition_pct_peak":92,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"A romantic partnership in which both parties have agreed that sexual or romantic activity with others outside the relationship is permitted.","english_term":"open relationship","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"Broad umbrella term encompassing swinging, polyamory, and don't-ask-don't-tell arrangements; widely understood across generations; less subculture-specific than either 'polyamory' or 'swinging.'"}
{"term":"cuckold","lang":"en","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"kink_paraphilia","subcategory":"cuckoldry","concept":"cuckoldry","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"archaic","crude":38,"technical":20,"recognition_pct_current":75,"recognition_pct_peak":80,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"A man whose partner has sex with other men; historically a term of humiliation and social shame, now also used for the consensual kink practice involving erotic arousal from the same scenario.","english_term":"cuckold","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"Derived from cuckoo bird behavior; the abbreviation 'cuck' had a spike as a political insult circa 2016; kink usage remains active online; the term straddles the honor/shame economy and kink categories depending on era and context."}
{"term":"cuckoldry","lang":"en","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"kink_paraphilia","subcategory":"cuckoldry","concept":"cuckoldry","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"archaic","crude":25,"technical":38,"recognition_pct_current":48,"recognition_pct_peak":58,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"The state or practice of being a cuckold; in modern use, the consensual kink in which a man's erotic arousal derives from his partner's sexual activity with other men.","english_term":"cuckoldry","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"More formal and archaic noun form than 'cuckold' or 'cucking'; appears in historical literature and academic or kink-community writing; the -ry suffix gives it a quasi-clinical or literary register."}
{"term":"hotwife","lang":"en","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"kink_paraphilia","subcategory":"cuckoldry","concept":"hotwife","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"subculture","crude":28,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":42,"recognition_pct_peak":42,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"A married woman who has sex with men other than her husband with his full knowledge and enthusiastic consent, framed as a source of shared erotic pleasure.","english_term":"hotwife","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"Distinct from cuckoldry in framing: hotwife arrangements emphasize the wife's desirability and the husband's pride and arousal rather than humiliation; recognized within CNM and kink communities; primarily an internet-era subculture term."}
{"term":"stag","lang":"en","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"kink_paraphilia","subcategory":"cuckoldry","concept":"stag_vixen","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"subculture","crude":10,"technical":8,"recognition_pct_current":22,"recognition_pct_peak":22,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"In the stag/vixen kink dynamic, the husband or male partner who takes confident pride in facilitating and watching his wife's sexual activity with other men.","english_term":"stag","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"Used in the 'stag/vixen' pairing as an explicit alternative framing to cuckold; emphasizes confidence and pride rather than humiliation; very niche, primarily found in online kink forums; 'stag' has many common non-sexual meanings that swamp this usage in general corpora."}
{"term":"voyeur","lang":"en","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"kink_paraphilia","subcategory":"voyeurism","concept":"voyeurism","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"clinical","crude":18,"technical":55,"recognition_pct_current":87,"recognition_pct_peak":87,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"A person who derives sexual pleasure from watching others engaged in intimate or sexual behavior.","english_term":"voyeur","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"French loanword (voyeur, 'one who sees'); clinical use implies non-consensual watching (DSM voyeuristic disorder); kink use may be fully consensual; the word is broadly understood in general culture and has a neutral-to-titillating register in lay usage."}
{"term":"exhibitionist","lang":"en","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"kink_paraphilia","subcategory":"exhibitionism","concept":"exhibitionism","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"clinical","crude":18,"technical":55,"recognition_pct_current":85,"recognition_pct_peak":85,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"A person who derives sexual pleasure from exposing themselves or performing sexually in front of others.","english_term":"exhibitionist","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"Clinical term spanning both DSM exhibitionistic disorder (non-consensual exposure) and consensual kink exhibitionism; also used metaphorically for attention-seeking behavior in non-sexual contexts, which slightly dilutes the sexual signal in general corpora."}
{"term":"sadomasochist","lang":"en","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"kink_paraphilia","subcategory":"sadomasochism","concept":"sadomasochism","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"clinical","crude":25,"technical":65,"recognition_pct_current":78,"recognition_pct_peak":78,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"A person who derives sexual arousal from both inflicting and receiving pain, humiliation, or domination.","english_term":"sadomasochist","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"Portmanteau of sadist (from Marquis de Sade) and masochist (from Leopold von Sacher-Masoch); abbreviated S&M or SM; largely subsumed into the broader BDSM framework in contemporary kink discourse, making 'sadomasochist' feel slightly clinical or dated compared to 'into BDSM.'"}
